An Electron file manager splits the work across three processes. The main process runs every file-system and shell operation, the preload script exposes a few named functions, and the renderer only draws the list and calls those functions.


Most file-manager tutorials you find for Electron date from 2016 to 2021. They turn on` nodeIntegration` , call` require('fs')` from the page, and reach for` remote` . Code written that way either breaks on current Electron or leaves a security hole open. This guide builds a working file browser using the current model: a sandboxed renderer, a narrow preload bridge, and main-process handlers that check every path they are given. If you haven’t built an Electron app before, read[Building Desktop Apps with Electron](https://blog.openreplay.com/building-desktop-apps-with-electron/) first.


## Key Takeaways


- The` shell` module does not work in a sandboxed renderer, so` shell.openPath` and` shell.trashItem` have to run in the main process behind an` ipcMain.handle` channel.
- ` shell.openPath` doesn’t reject on failure. It resolves with an error message, and only an empty string means the file opened.
- Node’s` fs.rename` overwrites an existing destination, so check that the target name is free before you rename.
- Each preload function should wrap exactly one IPC channel. A generic` invoke(channel, ...args)` lets any script in the page call every handler you register.
- Treat every path the renderer sends as untrusted. Resolve it in main, compare it against the folder the user picked, and refuse anything outside that folder.


## What You’ll Build: An Electron File Manager


The app does five things: lists a folder, opens subfolders and files, shows each entry’s size and modified date, renames entries, and moves them to the trash. The table shows where each operation runs.


Feature Runs in API IPC channel


Pick root folder Main` dialog.showOpenDialog`` dialog:pickFolder`


List folder Main` fs.readdir` +` fs.stat`` fs:list`


Open subfolder Renderer → Main` fs:list` again` fs:list`


Open file Main` shell.openPath`` shell:open`


Rename Main` fs.rename`` fs:rename`


Move to trash Main` shell.trashItem`` shell:trash`


## Project Setup and Secure webPreferences


Scaffold the project with[Electron Forge’s Vite template](https://www.electronforge.io/templates/vite) :


```text
npx   create-electron-app@latest   my-app   --template=vite
```


Forge labels its Vite support experimental. If you want the more conservative option, use` --template=webpack` .


According to the[Electron security checklist](https://www.electronjs.org/docs/latest/tutorial/security) , Node integration has been off by default since Electron 5, context isolation on since 12, and the sandbox on since 20. Set all three explicitly anyway, so nobody reading the code has to remember the defaults:


```text
mainWindow   =   new   BrowserWindow  ({
width  :   960  ,
height  :   640  ,
webPreferences  :   {
preload  :   path  .  join  (  __dirname  ,   '  preload.cjs  '  ),
contextIsolation  :   true  ,   // preload and page get separate JS worlds
nodeIntegration  :   false  ,   // no require() in the page
sandbox  :   true  ,            // renderer runs under Chromium's sandbox
},
});
// keep the template's existing loadURL / loadFile lines below this
```


Forge 8’s Vite template builds the preload script as` preload.cjs` . If your generated` main.js` uses a different filename, keep the one it already has.


Turning off context isolation also turns off the renderer sandbox, whatever the` sandbox` option says. A sandboxed preload can only` require('electron')` (or` electron/renderer` /` electron/common` ).[Process Sandboxing](https://www.electronjs.org/docs/latest/tutorial/sandbox) rules out Node built-ins,` Buffer` and local files, so the preload has to be a single file or bundled into one.


## Main Process: File-System and Shell Handlers


In an Electron file manager, all privileged work happens in the main process, in` src/main.js` . The[shell module](https://www.electronjs.org/docs/latest/api/shell) can be used in a renderer, but it won’t function in a sandboxed one. That means opening and trashing files go through IPC as well.


```text
const   {   app  ,   BrowserWindow  ,   ipcMain  ,   dialog  ,   shell   }   =   require  (  '  electron  '  );
const   path   =   require  (  '  node:path  '  );
const   fs   =   require  (  '  node:fs/promises  '  );


let   mainWindow   =   null  ;
let   rootDir   =   null  ;   // set only by the folder picker


function   assertSender  (  event  ) {
if (  BrowserWindow  .  fromWebContents  (  event  .  sender  )   !==   mainWindow  ) {
throw   new   Error  (  '  Untrusted sender  '  );
}
}


function   insideRoot  (  target  ) {
const   rel   =   path  .  relative  (  rootDir  ,   target  );
return   rel   ===   ''   ||
(  rel   !==   '  ..  '   &&   !  rel  .  startsWith  (  '  ..  '   +   path  .  sep  )   &&   !  path  .  isAbsolute  (  rel  ));
}


async   function   resolveInRoot  (  p  ) {
if (  !  rootDir  )   throw   new   Error  (  '  Pick a folder first  '  );
const   abs   =   await   fs  .  realpath  (  path  .  resolve  (  String  (  p  )));
if (  !  insideRoot  (  abs  ))   throw   new   Error  (  '  Path is outside the chosen folder  '  );
return   abs  ;
}


// Realpath the parent only, so rename/trash act on a symlink, not its target
async   function   resolveEntry  (  p  ) {
const   abs   =   path  .  resolve  (  String  (  p  ));
const   parent   =   await   resolveInRoot  (  path  .  dirname  (  abs  ));
return   path  .  join  (  parent  ,   path  .  basename  (  abs  ));
}


function   handle  (  channel  ,   fn  ) {
ipcMain  .  handle  (  channel  ,   async   (  event  ,   ...  args  )   =>   {
assertSender  (  event  );
return   fn  (  ...  args  );
});
}


handle  (  '  fs:list  '  ,   async   (  dir  )   =>   {
const   abs   =   await   resolveInRoot  (  dir   ??   rootDir  );
const   dirents   =   await   fs  .  readdir  (  abs  , {   withFileTypes  :   true   });
const   settled   =   await   Promise  .  allSettled  (  dirents  .  map  (  async   (  d  )   =>   {
const   full   =   path  .  join  (  abs  ,   d  .  name  );
const   st   =   await   fs  .  stat  (  full  );
return   {   name  :   d  .  name  ,   path  :   full  ,   isDir  :   st  .  isDirectory  (),
isLink  :   d  .  isSymbolicLink  (),   size  :   st  .  size  ,   mtimeMs  :   st  .  mtimeMs   };
}));
const   entries   =   settled  .  filter  ((  r  )   =>   r  .  status   ===   '  fulfilled  '  ).  map  ((  r  )   =>   r  .  value  )
.  sort  ((  a  ,   b  )   =>   (  b  .  isDir   -   a  .  isDir  )   ||   a  .  name  .  localeCompare  (  b  .  name  ));
return   {   dir  :   abs  ,   entries   };
});


handle  (  '  fs:rename  '  ,   async   (  target  ,   newName  )   =>   {
const   name   =   String  (  newName  ).  trim  ();
if (  !  name   ||   name   ===   '  .  '   ||   name   ===   '  ..  '   ||   name  .  includes  (  '  /  '  )   ||   name  .  includes  (  path  .  sep  )) {
throw   new   Error  (  '  Invalid file name  '  );
}
const   src   =   await   resolveEntry  (  target  );
const   dest   =   path  .  join  (  path  .  dirname  (  src  ),   name  );
if (  !  insideRoot  (  dest  ))   throw   new   Error  (  '  Path is outside the chosen folder  '  );
const   taken   =   await   fs  .  access  (  dest  ).  then  (()   =>   true  , ()   =>   false  );
if (  taken  )   throw   new   Error  (  `  "  ${  name  }  " already exists  `  );
await   fs  .  rename  (  src  ,   dest  );
});


handle  (  '  shell:open  '  ,   async   (  target  )   =>   {
const   err   =   await   shell  .  openPath  (  await   resolveInRoot  (  target  ));
if (  err  )   throw   new   Error  (  err  );
});


handle  (  '  shell:trash  '  ,   async   (  target  )   =>   {
const   abs   =   await   resolveEntry  (  target  );
if (  abs   ===   rootDir  )   throw   new   Error  (  '  Cannot trash the root folder  '  );
await   shell  .  trashItem  (  path  .  resolve  (  abs  ));
});
```


Here is what each part of the main-process code does:


- **Listing.**[fs.readdir with withFileTypes](https://nodejs.org/api/fs.html#fspromisesreaddirpath-options) returns` Dirent` objects.` fs.stat` adds the size and modification time.` Promise.allSettled` stops a single broken symlink from failing the whole listing.
- **Plain objects only.** Each entry is mapped to plain data because IPC uses the[Structured Clone algorithm](https://www.electronjs.org/docs/latest/tutorial/ipc#object-serialization) , and an` fs.Stats` instance would lose its methods on the way across.
- **Rename.**[Node’s fs.rename](https://nodejs.org/api/fs.html#fsrenameoldpath-newpath-callback) silently replaces` newPath` if it already exists, which is why the handler checks first. There is a small window between the check and the rename, which is acceptable for a desktop tool.
- **Open.**` shell.openPath` resolves with the error message if the open failed, and with an empty string otherwise. The handler converts a non-empty string into a thrown error.
- **Trash.**` shell.trashItem` moves the item to the platform’s trash instead of deleting it permanently. It rejects if there was an error while deleting the requested item. The` shell` docs require native separators, which is why the path goes through` path.resolve()` .


## Preload: A Small Named API


The preload script exposes five named functions to the renderer and nothing else. Older tutorials did it like this. Don’t:


```text
// Outdated pattern. Never ship this.
webPreferences  : {   nodeIntegration  :   true  ,   contextIsolation  :   false   }
const   fs   =   require  (  '  fs  '  );   // in the page
```


Here is the current pattern, in` src/preload.js` :


```text
const   {   contextBridge  ,   ipcRenderer   }   =   require  (  '  electron  '  );


contextBridge  .  exposeInMainWorld  (  '  electronAPI  '  , {
listDir  :   (  dir  )   =>   ipcRenderer  .  invoke  (  '  fs:list  '  ,   dir  ),
pickFolder  :   ()   =>   ipcRenderer  .  invoke  (  '  dialog:pickFolder  '  ),
openPath  :   (  p  )   =>   ipcRenderer  .  invoke  (  '  shell:open  '  ,   p  ),
rename  :   (  p  ,   newName  )   =>   ipcRenderer  .  invoke  (  '  fs:rename  '  ,   p  ,   newName  ),
trash  :   (  p  )   =>   ipcRenderer  .  invoke  (  '  shell:trash  '  ,   p  ),
});
```


Each preload function has its IPC channel name hard-coded. Never write` invoke: (...a) => ipcRenderer.invoke(...a)` , because it gives the page access to every handler you register. Also note that when a handler[registered with ipcMain.handle throws](https://www.electronjs.org/docs/latest/tutorial/ipc) , the renderer only gets the error’s` message` . Write messages a user can read. If an IPC call hangs or fails without explanation,[Debugging and Troubleshooting Common Electron Issues](https://blog.openreplay.com/debugging-troubleshooting-electron-issues/) explains how to tell main-process faults from renderer faults.


## Renderer: List, Open, Rename, Trash


The renderer only touches the DOM and` window.electronAPI` . File names can contain markup, so set them with` textContent` and never with` innerHTML` .


First, give the page something to render into. Replace the` <body>` of the template’s` index.html` with this markup, keeping the template’s` <script>` tag for` src/renderer.js` :


```text
<  body  >
<  header  >
<  button   id  =  "  pick  "  >  Choose folder  </  button  >
<  button   id  =  "  up  "  >  Up  </  button  >
<  span   id  =  "  status  "   role  =  "  status  "  ></  span  >
</  header  >
<  ul   id  =  "  list  "  ></  ul  >
<  dialog   id  =  "  rename-dialog  "  >
<  form   method  =  "  dialog  "  >
<  label  >  New name   <  input   id  =  "  rename-input  "   required  ></  label  >
<  button   value  =  "  cancel  "   formnovalidate  >  Cancel  </  button  >
<  button   value  =  "  ok  "  >  Rename  </  button  >
</  form  >
</  dialog  >
<  script   type  =  "  module  "   src  =  "  /src/renderer.js  "  ></  script  >
</  body  >
```


Electron doesn’t implement` window.prompt()` , so the rename box is a` <dialog>` . Then replace the contents of` src/renderer.js` :


```text
const   api   =   window  .  electronAPI  ;
const   list   =   document  .  querySelector  (  '  #list  '  );
const   status   =   document  .  querySelector  (  '  #status  '  );
const   renameDialog   =   document  .  querySelector  (  '  #rename-dialog  '  );
const   renameInput   =   document  .  querySelector  (  '  #rename-input  '  );
let   cwd   =   null  ;


function   askName  (  current  ) {
renameInput  .  value   =   current  ;
renameDialog  .  returnValue   =   ''  ;
renameDialog  .  showModal  ();
return   new   Promise  ((  resolve  )   =>   {
renameDialog  .  addEventListener  (  '  close  '  , ()   =>
resolve  (  renameDialog  .  returnValue   ===   '  ok  '   ?   renameInput  .  value   :   null  ), {   once  :   true   });
});
}


const   fmtSize   =   (  n  )   =>   {
const   u   =   [  '  B  '  ,   '  KB  '  ,   '  MB  '  ,   '  GB  '  ,   '  TB  '  ];   let   i   =   0  ;
while (  n   >=   1024   &&   i   <   u  .  length   -   1  ) {   n   /=   1024  ;   i  ++  ; }
return   `${  n  .  toFixed  (  i   ?   1   :   0  )  }   ${  u  [  i  ]  }`  ;
};


async   function   run  (  fn  ) {
try   {   status  .  textContent   =   ''  ;   await   fn  (); }
catch   (  err  ) {   status  .  textContent   =   err  .  message  ; }
}


async   function   load  (  dir  ) {
const   res   =   await   api  .  listDir  (  dir  );
cwd   =   res  .  dir  ;
list  .  replaceChildren  (  ...  res  .  entries  .  map  (  row  ));
}


function   button  (  label  ,   onClick  ) {
const   b   =   document  .  createElement  (  '  button  '  );
b  .  textContent   =   label  ;
b  .  addEventListener  (  '  click  '  , (  e  )   =>   {   e  .  stopPropagation  ();   run  (  onClick  ); });
return   b  ;
}


function   row  (  entry  ) {
const   li   =   document  .  createElement  (  '  li  '  );
const   cells   =   [  entry  .  isDir   ?   `${  entry  .  name  }  /  `   :   entry  .  name  ,
entry  .  isDir   ?   ''   :   fmtSize  (  entry  .  size  ),
new   Date  (  entry  .  mtimeMs  ).  toLocaleString  ()];
for (  const   text   of   cells  ) {
const   span   =   document  .  createElement  (  '  span  '  );
span  .  textContent   =   text  ;
li  .  append  (  span  );
}
li  .  append  (
button  (  '  Rename  '  ,   async   ()   =>   {
const   name   =   await   askName  (  entry  .  name  );
if (  name   &&   name   !==   entry  .  name  ) {   await   api  .  rename  (  entry  .  path  ,   name  );   await   load  (  cwd  ); }
}),
button  (  '  Trash  '  ,   async   ()   =>   {
if (  confirm  (  `  Move "  ${  entry  .  name  }  " to the trash?  `  )) {   await   api  .  trash  (  entry  .  path  );   await   load  (  cwd  ); }
}),
);
li  .  addEventListener  (  '  dblclick  '  , ()   =>
run  (()   =>   (  entry  .  isDir   ?   load  (  entry  .  path  )   :   api  .  openPath  (  entry  .  path  ))));
return   li  ;
}


document  .  querySelector  (  '  #up  '  ).  addEventListener  (  '  click  '  , ()   =>   run  (()   =>   load  (  `${  cwd  }  /..  `  )));
```


Double-clicking a folder lists its contents. Double-clicking a file passes it to the OS. The **Up** button sends` cwd/..` . Once you reach the root, main refuses the request and the status line displays the error.


## Folder Picker With dialog.showOpenDialog


The root folder comes only from a native dialog in main, and it is stored only in main. The renderer never sends a root. It sends paths, and main checks them against the root it already holds. Add this handler to` main.js` :


```text
handle  (  '  dialog:pickFolder  '  ,   async   ()   =>   {
const   {   canceled  ,   filePaths   }   =   await   dialog  .  showOpenDialog  (  mainWindow  , {
properties  :   [  '  openDirectory  '  ],
});
if (  canceled  )   return   null  ;
rootDir   =   await   fs  .  realpath  (  filePaths  [  0  ]);
return   rootDir  ;
});
```


In the[dialog API](https://www.electronjs.org/docs/latest/api/dialog) , the` openDirectory` property is what allows directories to be selected. Passing the window as the first argument attaches the dialog to it as a parent, which makes the dialog modal. The root is passed through` realpath` once here. That way, a symlink inside the folder that points somewhere else resolves outside the root and fails` insideRoot` .


Connect the picker in the renderer:


```text
document  .  querySelector  (  '  #pick  '  ).  addEventListener  (  '  click  '  , ()   =>   run  (  async   ()   =>   {
const   dir   =   await   api  .  pickFolder  ();
if (  dir  )   await   load  (  dir  );
}));
```


## Hardening: Paths, Senders, and CSP


Three controls make this Electron file browser safe to build on:


1. **Path containment.** Every handler passes renderer input through` resolveInRoot` or` resolveEntry` . Both resolve symlinks and use` path.relative` to reject anything that climbs out of the root or lands on another Windows drive.
2. **Sender validation.** The security checklist says to validate the sender of IPC messages, because any frame can send them.` assertSender` accepts calls only from` mainWindow` ’s` webContents` .
3. **Content Security Policy.** A page loaded from` file://` can’t receive CSP as an HTTP header, so add a meta tag to` index.html` :


```text
<  meta   http-equiv  =  "  Content-Security-Policy  "   content  =  "  default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'  "  >
```


Optionally, block navigation and new windows in main:


```text
mainWindow  .  webContents  .  on  (  '  will-navigate  '  , (  e  )   =>   e  .  preventDefault  ());
mainWindow  .  webContents  .  setWindowOpenHandler  (()   =>   ({   action  :   '  deny  '   }));
```


## Conclusion


You now have a secure Electron file manager. Main owns the file system and the root folder, the preload exposes five fixed functions, and the renderer can’t reach anything you didn’t hand it. Extend it the same way every time: one new handler in main, one new function in the preload, and the same path and sender checks on each. When you’re ready to ship, follow[Packaging and Distributing Electron Apps](https://blog.openreplay.com/packaging-distributing-electron-apps/) . If Electron’s footprint is a concern for this kind of tool, see[Comparing Electron and Tauri for Desktop Applications](https://blog.openreplay.com/comparing-electron-tauri-desktop-applications/) .


## FAQs


Why does prompt() throw an error in an Electron renderer?


Electron does not implement window.prompt(), so calling it throws an error instead of opening a dialog. The alert() and confirm() functions do work in Electron. To ask for a new file name, use an in-page text input or an HTML dialog element. You can also open a small modal BrowserWindow from the main process and send the entered value back over IPC.


How do I get the real path of a file dropped onto an Electron window?


Call webUtils.getPathForFile(file) in the preload script and expose it through contextBridge. Electron 32 removed the nonstandard File.path property from the Web File object, so code that reads file.path no longer gets a path. Send the returned path to a main-process handler and give it the same root-folder check as any other renderer input.


How can the file list refresh automatically when files change on disk?


Watch the folder being listed with fs.watch in the main process, and push a notification to the page with mainWindow.webContents.send. In the preload, wrap ipcRenderer.on so it calls the renderer's callback without the event object, because passing the event along exposes ipcRenderer to the page. Treat each event only as a signal to run fs:list again. Node reports just 'rename' and 'change', and the filename can be null.


